Obsidian Ribcage

Ingredients: ELECTROPOP + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Bass Guitar  
Creative Catalyst: The sound was reverse-engineered from a heat-warped cassette tape recovered from a 1989 municipality time capsule.

Obsidian Ribcage is a predatory, danceable style of dark pop that trades lush synths for hyper-processed, percussive bass guitar work and claustrophobic drum programming. The music contrasts pristine, melodic vocal hooks with a decaying, warbled low-end that sounds like it is actively corroding. It creates a tension between the immediate, physical urge of an underground club beat and a deeply unsettling, gothic dread.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 108–116 BPM. The pulse relies on a rigid, minimalist electro 4-on-the-floor kick, subverted by off-beat, hand-muted bass guitar scratches that simulate a mechanical heartbeat.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor locrian and harmonic minor modes. Key signatures favor C# minor and F minor, packed with unresolved tritone intervals and sudden, sharp pitch-bends.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Magnetic Wow-and-Flutter Emulation: All pitched synth leads are passed through analog tape-saturation units set to extreme azimuth tape-skewing, giving hooks a seasick, microtonal drift.
  2. Bi-Amped Bass Splitting: The bass guitar signal is split: one path drives a clean, rubbery sub-bass synth pedal, while the upper frequencies are slammed through high-gain distortion and a micro-delay to sound like cracking glass.
  3. Gated Grotto Reverbs: Vocals and snare snaps are treated with non-linear, ultra-short gated reverbs designed to simulate the dry, cold acoustic bounce of a concrete drainage bunker.
- Dynamics: A predatory crawl-to-crush dynamic; tracks typically start with an isolated, trembling bass riff and hushed vocals before exploding into dense, suffocating electro-pop chorus drops where the distorted bass overwhelms the mix.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged in late-2010s Leeds, where a circle of rogue audio archivists unburied a municipal time capsule planted in 1989 beneath an abandoned textile mill. Inside was a magnetic tape so heat-damaged that the synth-pop tracks recorded on it had melted into pitch-shifted, subterranean thuds punctuated by violent dropouts. Local bassists and bedroom producers tried to recreate this degraded artifact live, using active-pickup bass guitars plugged straight into cheap samplers. The resulting subculture—known as "Tape-Rotters"—grew out of damp basement venues like The Culvert. Fashion is strictly anti-photogenic: high-collared black rubber macs, smeared soot-black eye makeup, worn velvet boots, and silver jewelry crafted to look like bent audio cassette reels. Rituals include playing live sets under flickering overhead halogen work-lights while burning magnetic tape spools in ceramic dishes to fill the dancefloor with an acrid, ritualistic haze.

Typical Instruments
- Gould/Music Man Active Bass Guitar: The undisputed centerpiece, played strictly with heavy metal plectrums or aggressive thumb-slaps, run through envelope filters and pitch-shifters to act as the song's primary lead synth.
- E-mu Emulator II & LinnDrum: Used for dry, punchy 80s drum samples, pitched down dramatically to hit a industrial, metallic tone.
- Sequential Circuits Pro-One: Provides cold, biting bass-line reinforcement and high-register arpeggios that mimic emergency alarms.

For Fans Of: Boy Harsher, Bauhaus, Fever Ray, early Ladytron

